\subsection{How much can the sources help at most?}
\label{sec:res-ceiling}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../figures/fig4_ceiling}
\caption{Observed RMSE ratio of uniform~$\times$~difference IRT to uniform~$\times$~mean at $n=100$ against the prediction $\sqrt{1-q}$, one point per benchmark and split, where $q$ is the fraction of Bernoulli variance of the target's unsampled items explained by the predictor; left: the sources' item means (no target answers), right: a 2D IRT model fitted with the target's $n$ answers. The prediction is a reference, not a bound. Blue: the three main splits; orange: extrapolation by the top 10\% and 50\% of models; hollow: thin cells.}
\label{fig:ceiling}
\end{figure}

With a fixed predictor the difference estimator replaces the variance of the item outcomes by that of their residuals, so a predictor that explains a fraction $q$ of the variance of the unsampled outcomes should reach a ratio of $\sqrt{1-q}$ to uniform sampling when $n\ll N$.
Figure~\ref{fig:ceiling} compares this prediction with the observed ratio.
With the source-only predictor (left panel) the prediction is $0.93$, $0.94$ and $1.05$ (geometric mean over the 13 benchmarks) in interpolation, lineage and extrapolation, and the observed ratios $0.70$, $0.73$ and $0.85$ are below it in all three, because the difference estimator also uses the target's own answers, through the ability estimate and the residual correction.
With the IRT predictor fitted on the target's $n$ answers (right panel) the prediction is $0.81$, $0.82$ and $0.85$; the observed ratio is below it in interpolation ($0.70$) and lineage ($0.73$) and equals it in extrapolation ($0.85$).
The agreement in extrapolation is a coincidence of two effects, since the reference ignores the bias that remains there; with the top $50\%$ of models as targets the observed ratio is $0.97$ against $0.90$.
We read $\sqrt{1-q}$ as a reference for how much variance a predictor can remove when targets resemble the sources, not as a bound.
Near-chance files (GPQA, MuSR) have little variance to remove, and for the smallest files, where $n=100$ is up to $23\%$ of the items, the finite-population correction already lowers the variance of uniform sampling.

\subsection{Does a smaller error mean a better ordering?}
\label{sec:res-order}

Score error and ordering do not move together (Figure~\ref{fig:order} and Table~\ref{tab:ordering}, appendix).
The answer is conditional.
When targets resemble the sources, they buy a large variance reduction, about a fifth of the error of stratified sampling, which already uses the sources' item difficulties.
When every target is stronger than every source, predictors fitted on the sources are biased toward the source mean, and what remains over stratified sampling is at most a tenth; a difference correction reduces that bias without removing it, and a weight chosen on an inner split of the sources repairs it only in part: a larger-gap inner split, tried after the main result, helps the logistic predictor and hurts ridge.
The answer depends on the distance above the sources, on the number of source models, and, for ordering, is confined to interpolation and lineage.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
\emph{Baselines.}
The anchor-point, tinyBenchmarks-style and metabench-style methods are our re-implementations; the anchor clustering is light ($k$-means with one start and $25$ iterations); a stronger clustering, run on the seven leaderboard benchmarks only, changes the ratios by at most $0.10$ and no conclusion.
