\subsection{Where does the bias of model-based predictors sit?}
\label{sec:res-bias}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../figures/fig3_bias_by_score}
\caption{Signed error (predicted minus true full score, in points) against the target's position in standard deviations of the source models' full scores above (+) or below ($-$) their mean, $n=100$. Each line is the mean error over the non-thin benchmarks with targets in the bin, targets pooled across benchmarks (benchmarks with more targets weigh more); the band is its 95\% family-bootstrap interval. Bins with fewer than five benchmarks or 30 targets are not drawn. Extrapolation targets lie above every source by construction, so that panel has no bins below $+1$.}
\label{fig:bias}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:bias} shows that predictors fitted on the sources compress their estimates toward the source mean: the IRT model, calibrated regression and the anchor plug-in overestimate weak targets and underestimate strong ones, and in extrapolation the IRT model on a uniform design is $5.1$, $6.3$ and $9.7$ points too low in the bins one to two, two to three and above three standard deviations above the sources, and the anchor plug-in $4.9$, $7.4$ and $12.4$.
The sample-mean designs stay within about $0.7$ points of zero, as design-unbiased estimators should; the difference estimator, whose residual term is meant to remove this bias, reduces it to $0.8$, $2.0$ and $3.2$ points (uniform $\times$ difference IRT) but not to zero: the intervals exclude zero.
The variance, computed per target over subset draws, tells the same story (Table~\ref{tab:biasvar}, appendix): in extrapolation the IRT model has $0.27$ of the variance of uniform sampling but $4.56$ of its mean squared error ($4.29$ of it squared bias), whereas the difference estimator on a balanced design has $0.42$ of the variance and $0.69$ of the mean squared error.
The better methods cut the variance of uniform sampling by $40$--$60$\% in every split; whether that becomes an error reduction depends on the bias that remains.

The weight $\lambda$ of the correction is chosen on an inner split of the sources that mimics the outer split.
In interpolation this helps ($0.65$ $[0.63,0.67]$ tuned against $0.74$ $[0.73,0.76]$ for $\lambda=1$ on a balanced design); in extrapolation it does not: tuned and fixed $\lambda=1$ are equal on a uniform design ($0.85$ and $0.85$) and tuning is slightly worse on balanced ($0.82$ against $0.78$) and PPS designs ($0.85$ against $0.82$).
The inner targets, the strongest sources, lie only just above the remaining sources, so the nested split imitates the distance to the real targets only partially.
A larger gap (the top half of the sources as inner targets), tried after seeing this result as a diagnosis, lowers the error of the logistic and kNN difference estimators by $4$--$6\%$ (paired ratio to the main inner split $0.94$ $[0.92,0.95]$ for balanced $\times$ difference IRT) and raises that of ridge by $9\%$ ($1.09$ $[1.08,1.11]$; Table~\ref{tab:robust}), so the shrinkage depends on how the inner split is built, and we do not recommend the larger split from this evidence.
